><text color="#9643FC" creationdate="D:20250702103538+10'00'" flags="print,nozoom,norotate" inreplyto="7e3a3950-c18e-46e4-863a-e35c11b75a30" date="D:20250702103538+10'00'" name="449ad399-5ee2-4ede-ba28-34b335c3b81c" icon="Comment" page="12" rect="166.883316,420.942383,190.883316,444.942383" subject="Sticky Note" title="Alexander Gibson"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>In summary, evidence supporting the use of ultrasound technology, and particularly&#xD;the MuscleSound</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-10.0pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>® </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>proprietary technique, as a valid measure of muscle glycogen stores is&#xD;equivocal. In terms of its use as a research tool, two data sets involving laboratory-based&#xD;cycling protocols validated a correlation with measurements of the glycogen content of&#xD;a biopsy-derived mixed muscle sample, providing a measure of muscle glycogen from&#xD;0–100 in arbitrary units under controlled conditions.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="12" rect="595.276001,352.942383,779.276001,444.942383"
/></text
